In France, the election has traditionally been a technology-free experience: voters insulate themselves into the booth, putting out pre-printed paper in the envelope, bearing the name of the chosen candidate.
After the officials confirm the identity of the voter, the voter throws an envelope into the ballot box and writes his/her signature on the list of voters.
The rules of procedure of the French electoral law are quite strictly formalized by law.
Since 1988, ballot boxes have to be transparent so that voters and observers can witness that there are no envelopes in the box at the beginning of the voting and that envelopes other than those that have been properly accounted for and added by the voters with the right to vote are not included.
