When a small group of living beings (a small population) are separated from the main population from which they originate (for example, they move across a mountain range or river or move to a new island, and coming back is not easy), they often find themselves in a different environment than before.
There are different resources and different competitors in the new environment, so the new population needs different qualities than previously needed and has to adapt in order to be a strong competitor.
The original population has not changed at all, they still need the same adjustments as before.
As time passes, as the new population adapts to its new environment, they look less and less like another population.
Ultimately, after thousands or even millions of years, the two populations look so different that they cannot be called the same species.
Let's call this process species formation, which simply means the development of a new species. Species formation is an inevitable consequence of evolution and a very important part of it.
