In the savannah, it is difficult for a primate who has a digestive system similar to a human, with the help of plants available to meet the needs of its amino acids.
Moreover, if it fails to do so, it will have serious consequences: retardation of growth, malnutrition and, ultimately, death.
The most readily available plant sources would have been the proteins in leaf and vegetables, but it is easy for primates like us to digest only when cooked.
On the other hand, animal foods (ants, termites, eggs) are not only easily digestible, but also provide a large amount of proteins that contain all the necessary amino acids.
